### Leave out

- Start-ups and pre-revenue businesses: Zander's work is fixing and scaling what is already running, not building from scratch.
- Businesses under £1m turnover: the fee does not fit and the problems are different at that size.
- Anyone signalling they want a quick fix: if the profile or outreach response shows impatience with process, pass.
- National FM corporates (Mitie, Amey, Sodexo, CBRE, ISS and similar): too large, decision not with an owner.
- Property developers and house builders: construction sector but not contracting operations, different problem set.
- Coaches, consultants and consulting firms: title filters above handle this, double-check company name if unsure.
- Recruiters and recruitment agencies: exclude unless Zander specifically opens that market.
- Psychologists, therapists and wellbeing practitioners: caught by title filter, exclude at keyword level too.

- **800 to 1,500** Target list size in Sales Navigator before outreach begins (enough to run 12 months of sequenced activity without exhausting the pool)
- **Above 30%** Connection acceptance rate to aim for (below this, review the request message and title targeting first)
- **Above 10%** Reply rate of accepted connections to aim for (below this, review the follow-up message and opening hook)

> **Watch these two numbers in the first two weeks:** Run the first 30 to 50 connection requests before scaling. If acceptance sits below 30%, the job title or industry filters are pulling in the wrong people - tighten the keyword strings and recheck that headcount and sector filters are applied together, not separately. If acceptance is healthy but replies are below 10% of those accepted, the opening message is the problem, not the list. Fix the message before adding volume. Do not expand the list until both numbers are moving in the right direction.

===== linkedin:rewrite (65s, 8806 chars)
This rewrite replaces a blank profile with a page that earns immediate credibility with HVAC and M&E MDs: it leads with the margin problem they recognise, anchors on the Harlow & Pike result, and ends with a clear next step.

### Headline

**Headline option 1**

```text
Helping HVAC and M&E contractors fix margin bleed, free the MD from the middle, and get retentions moving | Former Operations Director, Harlow & Pike
```

_Formula: Transformation + audience. Lists all three pain points in plain contracting language. Recommended if Zander wants maximum relevance to the target buyer on first impression. Under 220 characters and sector-specific from the first word._

**Headline option 2**

```text
Job-by-job profit visibility for building services contractors, without the MD stuck in the middle of everything | HVAC division: £4.2m to £11.8m
```

_Formula: Outcome + constraint removed. Leads with what the owner wants (visibility, freedom) and includes the career proof number. Recommended if Zander wants the result to do the credibility work before anyone reads the About section._

**Headline option 3**

```text
Former Ops Director, Harlow & Pike Building Services | Coaching HVAC and M&E MDs to stop the margin bleed and get out of the weeds
```

_Formula: Authority + specificity. Leads with the role and the employer, then states the outcome plainly. Good if Zander is connecting with people who already know Harlow & Pike or the building services world in the North of England._

> **Recommendation:** Use Headline option 1. It names the three problems the target buyer carries every day. The pain language ('margin bleed', 'MD from the middle', 'retentions moving') will stop the right person and filter out everyone else. The career anchor is in the About section where there is room to give it context.

### About

**About section**

```text
You're winning the work. The lads are busy. Turnover's up. So why isn't the money there at the end of the year?

That's the question I kept hearing when I was inside building services, and it's the question I hear every week now from MDs running HVAC and M&E firms across the North of England and the Midlands.

The answer is almost always the same three things: margin bleeding out on jobs before anyone's caught it, the MD still fielding every site call because no management layer is actually working, and retentions sitting on the aged debtors report for months that nobody's properly chasing.

I know those problems because I lived them. I spent my career inside building services as a Project Engineer, then Contracts Manager, then Operations Director at Harlow & Pike, where I led the HVAC division from £4.2m to £11.8m over five years. We got there by shifting the model from one-off installs to planned maintenance contracts and keeping a tight grip on job pricing through every stage of growth. I've sat in job reviews where the numbers told a different story to the quote. I've dealt with retention debt that sat unchased for too long. I've been the person trying to build a management layer underneath an MD who couldn't let go, because I understood why they couldn't.

I now work with MDs and owners of building services contractors to fix exactly those three things. My work is operational and numbers-based from week one. I sit in on job reviews and pricing discussions early, find where the margin is going, and then build a weekly numbers rhythm that gives you sight of live jobs before month-end rather than after.

This is for you if:
- You run an HVAC, M&E, electrical or FM contracting business turning over £1m to £20m
- You're winning work but the profit isn't where it should be and you can't tell which jobs are killing it
- You're still the first call for every site issue and you're exhausted with it
- You've tried an estimator, new software, or a consultant's report, and the margin is still bleeding

I take on a maximum of eight clients and I turn down quick-fix seekers at the first conversation. If you want someone who'll get into the numbers with you and already knows the difference between a PM contract and a one-off install, I'd be happy to have a straight conversation.

The BOSS Scorecard takes three minutes and shows you which part of the business is capping your profit right now. Link below. Or message me directly and we'll talk.
```

