### The first issue

**Issue 1**

```text
Subject: You're winning the work. So where's the profit going?

If your turnover's up but the bottom line tells a different story, you're not alone.

Most HVAC and M&E contractors I talk to have had a decent year on paper. Work on the board, the lads busy, new contracts signed. But sit down with the monthly P&L and there's a gap between what the business billed and what actually landed. Not a small gap either.

The instinct is to blame pricing. So they bring in an estimator, tighten up the tenders, and wait for the margins to improve. They usually don't. Not much, anyway.

Here's why.

The bleed almost never happens at quote stage. It happens in the field: variations that don't get priced up properly, labour hours that run over, materials that get ordered twice because nobody's tracking live job costs against the original tender. By the time the final account's done, the job that looked healthy at 12% margin has come in at four or five. Sometimes less.

At Harlow & Pike, when I led the HVAC division, we had the same problem. Revenue was growing but the margin wasn't keeping pace. We started running a simple weekly job cost review - nothing complicated, just a look at live jobs against what we'd priced, before month-end, not after. Within a few months we could see exactly which jobs were drifting and why, and we could do something about it while the job was still live.

That's what gave us the confidence to grow. We took the division from £4.2m to £11.8m over five years, and we held the margin through it because we had visibility on live jobs, not just on the P&L three weeks after the damage was done.

The practical action for this fortnight: pull out the last three jobs that came in below the margin you tendered at. Don't go to the accountant for this. Go to the job files. Look at where the hours went versus what you priced, and whether every variation was captured and billed. You don't need software to do this. You need an hour and a clear head.

What you find will tell you more about where your profit's going than six months of P&Ls.

If you want to talk through what you find, or you want to know how a weekly numbers rhythm works in practice, I offer a free one-hour session for building services MDs. No pitch. We look at the numbers together and I tell you honestly what I see.

Book a time here: [value session link]

Zander Demo
The Contractor's Profit Practice
[email address]
```

