- **12–16** Discovery calls needed (assuming 25–33% conversion)
- **60–80** Conversations needed to book those calls

> **Why four clients, not six:** At £1,950 a month, four clients brings in £7,800. Six would clear £11,700. The plan targets four first because the practice is new, your proof base is career-level rather than client-level, and a slower build lets you deliver properly and collect early results. Once you have two to three clients paying and progressing, raise the target and the price. The numbers above are built from your stated minimum fee and capacity of eight. If you sign clients at a higher rate, the call and conversation targets drop.

### Week by week

- **Weeks 1–2:** Foundations locked. LinkedIn rewritten and live. Profit Coach page live and pointed at your calendar. Practice email set up. BOSS Scorecard link confirmed and tested. Value session named and the 45-minute structure written. Prospect list of 100 MDs and owners built from HVAC, M&E, and electrical contracting businesses in the North and Midlands: LinkedIn search, trade directories, contacts you already know. First ten connection requests sent by end of week two.
- **Weeks 2–4:** First campaign live. Connector campaign running daily on LinkedIn: 10 connection requests sent each working day, accepted connections followed up with the margin bleed message. Target: 200 requests sent, 50 accepted, 10 replies. Your prospect list grows to 300 names. First pieces of content posted: two posts in week three, two in week four, all drawing on job costing and pricing stories from Harlow & Pike. Replies handled same day on Mondays and Thursdays. First call booked before end of week four.
- **Weeks 4–8:** Calls every week, first client signed. Two to three discovery calls a week on Monday and Thursday afternoons. Value sessions run for the strongest prospects. First client signed by week six at the latest. Campaign rhythm holds: 10 requests a day, follow-ups sent, replies handled. Content stays at four posts a month. After each value session, note what question or objection came up and write the next post around it. Track your numbers weekly: requests, accepted, replies, calls, sessions, clients.
- **Weeks 8–12:** Second angle tested, price reviewed, referrals asked for. Test a second outreach angle aimed at owner-dependency rather than margin bleed. See which pulls more replies. With two to three clients in, have the honest conversation about price: if you are filling quickly, the next client pays more. Send your first newsletter issue to the list you have built. Ask your first signed client for one referral introduction: a specific ask to a named type of person, not a general request. Review your pipeline numbers and set the target for the next quarter.

### Weekly numbers

| Activity | Per week |
| --- | --- |
| LinkedIn connection requests sent | 50 (10 each working day) |
| Follow-up messages sent to accepted connections | All accepted, within 48 hours |
| Replies handled (prospect conversations) | All replied to on same day, Mondays and Thursdays |
| Discovery calls held | 2–3 |
| Value sessions run | 1–2 (from week 5 onwards) |
| LinkedIn posts published | 1 (4 per month minimum) |
| Prospect list updated | 20–30 new names added |
| Pipeline reviewed | Once, Friday morning, 20 minutes |

### This week

1. **Lock the foundations in one sitting.** Update your LinkedIn headline and about section using the copy in your blueprint. Confirm your Profit Coach page is live. Set up your practice email. Test your BOSS Scorecard link end to end: click it, complete it, check the result lands correctly. Book a 30-minute slot later this week to do nothing but this. If it is not done by Friday, nothing else in the plan moves.
2. **Build your first prospect list.** Search LinkedIn for MDs and owners of HVAC contractors, M&E firms, and electrical contractors in the North of England and Midlands with 15 to 50 employees. Add contacts you already know from your time at Harlow & Pike: former clients, subcontractors, suppliers, peers from the industry. Aim for 100 names by end of week. Name, job title, company, LinkedIn URL. A spreadsheet is fine. This list is your pipeline.
3. **Send your first ten connection requests.** Pick the ten strongest names from your list: people you have a natural reason to connect with, or where the fit is obvious. Send a short, direct connection request with no pitch. Something like: 'Hi Mike, I spent years in HVAC contracting as Contracts Manager then Ops Director at Harlow & Pike. I come across a lot of MDs in building services with the same headaches around margin and retentions. Worth connecting.' Ten sent before Friday. Not nine. Ten.

> **Your most likely constraint: proof at the coaching level:** Your career result at Harlow & Pike is strong and specific. But Mike, the MD you are selling to, is sceptical of outside help. He has been burned before. Your Harlow & Pike story answers the credibility objection well, but it is career proof, not client proof. The moment a prospect asks 'who have you coached and what happened?' you need an answer. Spot it early: if you are getting replies and calls but not converting to signed clients, this is almost certainly why. Fix it by running your first two or three value sessions as paid diagnostics or by offering the first month at a structured trial rate, so you collect a specific result quickly. Log every result with a from, a to, and a timeframe. One real client result, properly recorded, changes the conversion rate across every future call.
